          %p
            These traditional formulations are geared towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
            (as opposed to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              The goal is to get closer to a systematic theory of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#GukovWitten08"}> Gukov-Witten 08, p.4
              )
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            the conceptual issues become even more severe. For example the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
            called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            is a core ingredient in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            but the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
            (see there for more) poses famous open problems – of which of course that there isn’t yet a comprehensive theory of what this even means is not the least.
          %h2#QuantizationAsAnIndexMap Quantization as an index map
          %p
            Here we survey aspects of quantization which give rise to an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            map or something similar, that is, a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"} Nuiten 13
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
          %p The general pattern here is this:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
                part of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
                is really part of the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation in generalized cohomology
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %li
              %p
                The (gauge coupling-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
                induces a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
                in some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                which serves as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twist
                for the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
                in which the above
                = succeed "/push-forward" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
                itself is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
          %h3#path_integral_by_integration_against_the_wiener_measure Path integral by integration against the Wiener measure
          %p
            For instance the archetypical example of the quantization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged particle
            propagating on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            proceeds (in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotated
            form as it appears in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            for corresponding QFT) like so: write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi D
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\exp(-S_{kin}(\gamma)) D\gamma]
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wiener+measure"} Wiener measure
            on paths in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            of the time evolution propagator of the quantum particle is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            (see there for more)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi in
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi out
                      %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi D
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{\gamma \in P_{x_{in}, x_{out}}X} tra_{\nabla}(\gamma) \; [\exp(- S_{kin}(\gamma)) D\gamma]
          %p
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            functional (of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            that models the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wiener+measure"} Wiener measure
          %h3#quantization_by_partition_functionindex_in_supersymmetric_quantum_mechanics Quantization by partition function/index in supersymmetric quantum mechanics
          %p
            More generally, for given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_\omega, \nabla)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            is, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            admits a compatible
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+Dirac+operator"} spin^c Dirac operator
            coupled to this bundle, hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo =
                  %mi index
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{H} = index( D_{\nabla} )
                  \\,.
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometric%20quantization#AsIndexOfSpinCDiracOperator"} geometric quantization – As Index of the Spin^c Dirac operator
          %p
            Here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            represents a class in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-homology"} K-homology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D] \in KK(X, \mathbb{C})
          %p
            and this is now the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"}> measure
            ” against which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            class
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [L_\omega] \in KK(\mathbb{C}, X)
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            is being integrated, by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            map hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi KK
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\mathcal{H}] \simeq
                  (\mathbb{C} \stackrel{L_\omega}{\to} C(X) \stackrel{D}{\to} \mathbb{C} ) \in KK(\mathbb{C}, \mathbb{C})
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            this is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of an auxiliary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            system with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi sTr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi t
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi t
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{H} \simeq sTr( \exp(-t D^2) ) \;\;\;\; \forall t \gt 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            The appearance of an
            %em auxiliary
            and already quantized field theory here may seem circular, but is in fact part of a deeper pattern of quantization by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            of higher dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theories"} topological field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            associated with the original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization+of+2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} extended geometric quantization of 2d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            arises this way as the quantization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            2d QFT regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horava-Witten+theory"} Horava-Witten theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"} Nuiten13
          %h3#cohomological_formulation_in_bvformalism Cohomological formulation in BV-formalism
          %p
            The choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            in the above is really the choice of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            (see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
            for more) which exhibits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation in generalized cohomology
            by a combined
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah+duality"} Atiyah duality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+isomorphism"} Thom isomorphism
          %p
            The analogue of such a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            is the choice of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            that identified the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-complex"} BV-complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "BV-BRST+formalism#HomologicalIntegration"} BV-BRST formalism – Homological integration
          %p
            Again, here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action+functional"} kinetic action functional
            is part of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-operator"} BV-operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            is now the analog of the orientability condition. Homological BV-quantization is then obtain by assing to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            of this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-operator"} BV-operator
          %p
            This is the setup in which one can derive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            rules form cohomologicalquantization, see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Feynman+diagram#ReferencesInHomologicalBVQuantization"} Feynman diagram – Refereces – In homological BV-quantization
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+harmonic+oscillator"} quantum harmonic oscillator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+the+2-sphere"} quantization of the 2-sphere
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+loop+groups"} quantization of loop groups
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong quantization
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+quantization"} Moyal quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"}> geometric quantization
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+by+push-forward"} geometric quantization by push-forward
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+quantization"}> Weyl quantization
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born-Jordan+quantization"} Born-Jordan quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+quantization"} stochastic quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} semiclassical approximation
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light-cone+gauge+quantization"} light-cone gauge quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+via+the+A-model"} quantization via the A-model
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+quantization"} BV quantization
            %li
              %p
                overview:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reductions+deformations+resolutions+in+physics"} reductions deformations resolutions in physics
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"}> Nik Weaver
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Quantization
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/Mathematical-Quantization/Weaver/p/book/9781584880011"} ISBN 9781584880011
            %li
              %p
                S. Twareque Ali, Miroslav Engliš,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantization Methods: A Guide for Physicists and Analysts
                %strong 17
                (2005) 391-490 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0405065"}> arXiv:math-ph/0405065
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Specifically on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %ul
            %li#BatesWeinstein
              Sean Bates,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+the+geometry+of+quantization"} Lectures on the geometry of quantization
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.berkeley.edu/~alanw/GofQ.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            See also references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            A proposal for a full formalization of the notion of quantization for “finite” theories such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.0731"}> arXiv:0905.0731
              )
          %p
            A historical discussion by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zoran+%C5%A0koda"} one
            of the
            = succeed "labizants" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathlight.wordpress.com/2010/03/19/quantization"} mathlight:quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
          %ul
            %li#HenneauxTeitelboim
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantization+of+Gauge+Systems"} Quantization of Gauge Systems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Reshetikhin"}> Nikolai Reshetikhin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on quantization of gauge systems
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1008.1411"} arxiv/1008.1411
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-method" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+via+the+A-model"} quantization via the A-model
          %ul
            %li#GukovWitten08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergey+Gukov"}> Sergey Gukov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Branes and quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.0305"} arxiv/0809.0305
          %p
            The perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric
            pre-quantization as a canonical construction in higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Rogers"} Chris Rogers
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Higher+geometric+prequantum+theory"} Higher geometric prequantum theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.0236"}> arXiv:1304.0236
              )
          %p
            A discussion of quantization as cohomological/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+prequantum+field+theory"} higher prequantum field theory
            is in
          %ul#Nuiten13
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"}> Joost Nuiten
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Nuiten"} Cohomological quantization of local boundary prequantum field theory
              Master thesis, Utrecht 2013
          %p
            Some discussion of quantization in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Kunji Nakayama,
              %em Sheaves in Quantum Topos Induced by Quantization
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1109.1192"}> arXiv:1109.1192
              )
          %p Joint discussion of a variety of quantization schemes:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joshua+Lackman"}> Joshua Lackman
              ,
              %em Geometric Quantization Without Polarizations
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01513"}> arXiv:2405.01513
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
